   "importance": 4,
   "certainty": "excavated",
   "summary": "A colossal hall 487 x 60 m of opus incertum concrete, its 294 piers carrying 50 parallel barrel-vaulted aisles that stepped down the slope toward the Tiber, the earliest large concrete building in Rome. It appears on the Severan Marble Plan inscribed ...]LIA. Traditionally the Porticus Aemilia warehouse of the Emporium; Cozza and Tucci (2006) identified it instead as the Navalia, the shipsheds of the Republican fleet, an identification still debated.",
   "phases": [
    {
     "y": -174,
     "t": "rebuilt by the censors Q. Fulvius Flaccus and A. Postumius Albinus (Livy 41.27.8)"
    },
    {
     "y": 100,
     "t": "used and partly rebuilt as warehouses in the Empire"
    }
   ],
   "sources": [
    {
     "a": "Livy",
     "w": "Ab urbe condita",
     "r": "35.10.12; 41.27.8"
    }
   ],
   "scholarship": [
    "Richardson (1992)",
    "Cozza and Tucci, 'Navalia', Archeologia Classica 57 (2006)",
    "Arata and Felici, 'Porticus Aemilia, navalia o horrea?', Archeologia Classica 62 (2011)",
    "Rodriguez Almeida, Forma Urbis Marmorea (1981)"
   ],
   "note": "Centre and long-axis bearing (46.7 deg) from OSM (Porticus Aemilia remains, Via Rubattino/Via Branca); front faces the river (NW, 316.7). Aisles run perpendicular to the river; 50 aisles x ~8.3 m plus walls. The excavated segment is near the middle of the length."
  },
